# S12.1 review: crash durability and result provenance

## Error image

The recovery guard previously treated any existing deployment result as a
successful completion, even if that file belonged to the backed-up pre-state.
Atomic writers fsynced file content but did not fsync the containing directory
after rename, leaving the exactly-once marker name vulnerable to power loss.

## Cause

The result lacked an explicit binding to the current attempt start timestamp,
and the recovery path checked only file existence. The atomic helpers stopped
after `os.replace`, which does not by itself establish durable directory
metadata. A subsequent review also established that the lock target lived in a
checkout-replaceable inode, backup payload files had no explicit fsync, and a
new state directory entry was not persisted in its parent.
The following review then identified root-owned Git metadata risk, non-durable
rollback unlink/symlink metadata, and a missing standalone simulator import
path.
The final P2 review found that pre-existing backup ancestors also require an
ownership/write-permission contract so another account cannot rename a durable
backup after the attempt marker.
A later review identified crash-stale Git transaction locks and ref drift from
release fetches as two remaining obstacles to exact rollback.
The next P1 review demonstrated that explicit `--git-dir` or equivalent
environment selectors must also participate in active-process detection.
The final P1 review found that `/opt/tu1nz_repos` is intentionally owned and
writable by `chatops`, so a backup below that tree can never satisfy the secure
root-only ancestry contract.
The subsequent P2 review found that two declared network gates were present in
the manifest but not yet enforced by both freeze validation and simulation.
The next P1 review confirmed that both shared `/var/log/tausendunde1nz` and
`/var/lib/tausendunde1nz` ancestors are group-writable and also demonstrated
that a crash-interrupted checkout can leave tracked and untracked worktree
fragments after stale lock removal.
The following review identified three remaining effective-contract gaps:
pre-existing systemd drop-ins, pre-existing enablement links, and callback
fields that were documented but not all freeze-validated.
The next P2 review found a vacuous all-false hard-gate check and a mutable
registered simulator scenario that could rewrite an under-threshold decision.
The following P2 review found that the real-user Sandbox final-posture flag was
not yet exact and that a mutable session result could impersonate authoritative
provider state during replay.
The next review demonstrated chained relative Git `-C` semantics and found that
partial credential-object validation could allow unknown value-bearing fields.

## Correction

The success result now binds the exact current backup path and attempt start
timestamp. Recovery blocks only on an exact valid binding; stale or malformed
results fall through to the existing rollback-only path. Atomic JSON and file
installation fsync both file content and the containing directory. Regression
tests cover exact result binding, malformed/stale handling and both fsync
classes. The transaction lock now uses the stable repository-parent directory
outside both worktrees. Backup payload, backup-directory and backup-parent
fsyncs complete before return, and every newly created private directory
component is persisted in its parent before mutations begin.
Every working-checkout Git process now runs as `chatops`; root streams bundle
bytes through already-open backup descriptors. Rollback deletion and symlink
operations fsync their parent directories, and the direct simulator CLI adds
the frozen source root before importing the package simulator.
Backup creation and recovery now validate every existing component through `/`
as root-owned, non-symlink directories without group/world write bits.
Recovery rejects active checkout Git processes, clears only strictly validated
stale lockfiles, and fsyncs those deletions. Release fetches never write
`FETCH_HEAD` or remote-tracking refs; the sole imported freeze-tag ref is
recorded and restored exactly.
Git activity detection now resolves cwd, CLI selectors and relevant `GIT_*`
environment paths; unreadable active Git processes block recovery
conservatively.
Backup and state now share a dedicated private root below `/etc/tu1nz`, whose
documented ancestry is root-owned and not group/world-writable. The strict
ancestor validation remains unchanged.
Freeze validation and the executable simulator now require `https_only=true`
and `redirect_to_unknown_host_allowed=false`; focused negative mutations prove
that neither insecure transport nor an unknown-host redirect can be frozen.
Rollback uses forced detached/attached checkouts and removes only non-ignored
untracked fragments after the pre-state proved clean, then revalidates the
exact commit, tree, branch tips, managed refs and clean status.
Preflight now rejects all applicable systemd drop-in files and every unexpected
enablement state. The installed effective unit must have empty `DropInPaths`
and be exactly static before start. Freeze validation and simulation bind the
entire callback object, including method, loopback bind, 16 KiB body limit,
public HTTPS URL and Sandbox signature contract.
Runtime source validation and simulation now require the exact hard-gate key
set as well as false values. Each session retains an independent immutable
registered-scenario binding; mutation of the same registered object is rejected
before trigger or authenticated result fetch and is exercised explicitly.
The full final-posture object is now exact, including real-user Sandbox=false.
Authoritative accepted results are held in a private registration map; a
pre-seeded or later-mutated public session result is rejected and covered by a
dedicated under-threshold-to-verified forgery journey.
Git repository selectors now apply `-C` options in order and resolve subsequent
relative paths from the prior effective selector. The entire credential object
is exact in freeze, runtime-source and simulator validation, so unknown fields
or altered references are rejected.

## Rollback plan

This is source-only before the immutable runtime freeze. Revert the amended
Control commit if validation fails. No server state or credentials were
changed while applying this correction.
